---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";
import { homes } from "../../lib/cases";
import { sites } from "../../lib/model";
import { siteConfig } from "../../site-config";

const climate = (id: string) => sites.find((s) => s.id === id)!.climate;
const fmt = (v: number) => v.toLocaleString("en-AU", { maximumFractionDigits: 0 });
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Five clients, five homes"
  description="Every student advises one of five households applying to the loan programme. The homes are fixed existing properties; the climates are real; the families are fictional."
  wide
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <p class="fiction">
    <strong>Teaching fiction.</strong> The households, their properties and the specialists who
    measure them are invented for this course. The climates behind them are Bureau of Meteorology
    long-term statistics, and every engineering method applied to them is real.
  </p>

  <p>
    You choose one home in week 1 and work on it all semester. Every home comes with the same kit:
    an intake dossier with scaled plans in week 1 (Release A), and a specialist team's findings
    after a fictional measurement year in week 7 (Release B). Every student who chooses a home
    receives the same Release B, whatever their own measurement programme proposed.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>There are only these five.</strong> You cannot propose another site, substitute land,
    or enlarge a roof or a plot. What you can change is set out on each dossier and on the{" "}
    <a href={withBase("/method/")}>method page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Choose a home</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={3}>
    {homes.map((h) => (
      <Card title={`${h.short}: the ${h.family} household`} href={`/clients/${h.slug}/`}>
        <p>{h.dwelling}.</p>
        <p class="pri">{h.dossier.priorities[0]}</p>
      </Card>
    ))}
  </CardGrid>

  <h2>The five side by side</h2>
  <Figure
    caption="Plot, growing and roof areas are computed from each plan; climate is Bureau of Meteorology long-term statistics for the named station. Brisbane's shared-garden allocation is common property and is shown separately, never added to the lot."
    basis="sourced"
    source="BoM stations 070014, 015590, 040214, 023000, 014015 [S39]."
  >
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Home</th>{homes.map((h) => <th scope="col">{h.short}</th>)}</tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Client</th>{homes.map((h) => <td>{h.family}</td>)}</tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Whole plot</th>{homes.map((h) => <td>{h.parcel_m2 >= 10000 ? `${h.parcel_m2 / 10000} ha` : `${fmt(h.parcel_m2)} m²`}</td>)}</tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Designated growing area</th>{homes.map((h) => <td>{fmt(h.areas.growing_envelope_m2)} m²{h.areas.shared_allocation_m2 ? ` + ${h.areas.shared_allocation_m2} m² shared` : ""}</td>)}</tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Roof plan area</th>{homes.map((h) => <td>{fmt(h.areas.roof_plan_m2)} m²</td>)}</tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Connected today</th>{homes.map((h) => <td>{fmt(h.areas.roof_connected_now_m2)} m²</td>)}</tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Existing storage</th>{homes.map((h) => <td>{h.areas.existing_storage_kl} kL</td>)}</tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Other water</th>{homes.map((h) => <td>{h.dossier.bore_allocation_kl ? `bore, ${h.dossier.bore_allocation_kl} kL/yr` : h.mains ? "mains" : "carted"}</td>)}</tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Station</th>{homes.map((h) => <td>{h.station}</td>)}</tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Mean annual rain</th>{homes.map((h) => <td>{fmt(climate(h.id).annual_rain_mm)} mm</td>)}</tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Rain in driest four months</th>{homes.map((h) => <td>{(climate(h.id).driest_4_months_share * 100).toFixed(1)} %</td>)}</tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Annual ETo</th>{homes.map((h) => <td>{fmt(climate(h.id).annual_eto_mm)} mm</td>)}</tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Care available</th>{homes.map((h) => <td>{h.dossier.maintenance_h_week} h/week</td>)}</tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Figure>

  <p>
    The largest <em>designated growing area</em> is Darwin's 500 m² or so; the largest <em>whole plot</em> is
    the 2 ha Alice Springs parcel, most of which is retained vegetation. Neither number says what a
    household can feed itself from. That depends on the crop, the water, the soil, the hours and
    the season, and it is worked out home by home in weeks 4 and 9.
  </p>

  <h2>Why these five</h2>
  <p>
    They span a cool inland suburb, an arid rural block, a subtropical townhouse, a Mediterranean
    suburb and a tropical wet-dry block, so between them they cover the problems the course teaches:
    frost, water scarcity, space and approvals, seasonal mismatch, and storage timing. No home is
    the easy one. Each leads two teaching weeks, and every week from 2 to 11 compares all five.
  </p>
  <p>
    Realistic parameters cannot make these climates equally hard, and the course does not pretend
    to. Fairness comes from the targets: each client's are set from the same model so that a
    feasible option exists at every home with a comparable margin, and the assessment rewards
    reasoning about <em>your</em> client, not a number compared across homes.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .fiction {
    padding: 0.8rem 1rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--at-primary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .pri {
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }

  th[scope="row"] {
    text-align: left;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  td {
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
</style>
